{"format":"studypack","version":1,"title":"Artificial Intelligence","course":"Artificial Intelligence","level":"Year 4","createdAt":"2026-11-12","includesBackground":false,"topics":[{"title":"Introduction to Artificial Intelligence","coverage":"full","gaps":[],"explanation":"Artificial intelligence is a field of computer science focused on building systems that can perform tasks normally requiring human intelligence. Most modern systems are narrow AI, designed for single specific tasks, whereas general AI does not yet exist. The field includes machine learning, natural language processing, computer vision, and robotics.","example":"recommending videos or detecting fraud in bank transactions","mnemonic":"","glossary":[{"term":"Artificial Intelligence","meaning":"The field of computer science that builds systems able to perform tasks that normally need human intelligence."},{"term":"Narrow AI","meaning":"AI systems designed for one specific task, such as recommending videos."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is Artificial Intelligence?","back":"The field of computer science that builds systems able to perform tasks that normally need human intelligence."},{"front":"What is narrow AI?","back":"AI systems designed for one specific task."},{"front":"Does general AI exist?","back":"No, a system that could learn any task a human can does not yet exist."},{"front":"What does computer vision allow?","back":"Computers to interpret images and video."},{"front":"What does robotics combine?","back":"AI with machines that act in the physical world."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What best describes most AI systems today?","options":["General AI","Narrow AI","Conscious AI","Human-level AI"],"answer":1,"why":"The notes state that most AI systems today are narrow AI, designed for one specific task."},{"q":"Which area allows computers to interpret images and video?","options":["Robotics","Natural language processing","Computer vision","Machine learning"],"answer":2,"why":"The notes state that computer vision allows computers to interpret images and video."},{"q":"What does machine learning let systems do?","options":["Learn patterns from data","Act in the physical world","Translate text manually","Think like a person"],"answer":0,"why":"The notes state that machine learning lets systems learn patterns from data."},{"q":"Which of the following does not yet exist according to the notes?","options":["Narrow AI","General AI","Machine learning","Computer vision"],"answer":1,"why":"The notes state that general AI, a system that could learn any task a human can, does not yet exist."}],"background":"","source":{"pages":[],"excerpt":"Artificial intelligence (AI) is the field of computer science that builds systems able to perform tasks that normally need human intelligence, such as recognising speech, understanding language, making decisions and translating text.","verified":true},"id":"t1"},{"title":"Overview of Machine Learning","coverage":"partial","gaps":["supervised learning","unsupervised learning","reinforcement learning"],"explanation":"Machine learning is a branch of AI where computers learn from examples rather than programmer-written rules. In traditional programming, developers write rules and provide data to get answers. In machine learning, developers provide data and correct answers, and the computer discovers the rules itself, storing them in a model.","example":"","mnemonic":"","glossary":[{"term":"Machine Learning","meaning":"A branch of AI in which a computer learns from examples instead of following rules written by a programmer."},{"term":"Model","meaning":"The storage place where the rules learned by a machine learning system are kept."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is machine learning?","back":"A branch of AI in which a computer learns from examples instead of following rules written by a programmer."},{"front":"How do traditional programming and machine learning differ regarding rules?","back":"In traditional programming, a developer writes rules; in machine learning, the computer works out the rules by itself."},{"front":"Where are the rules learned by machine learning stored?","back":"In a model."},{"front":"What are the three main types of machine learning mentioned?","back":"Supervised learning, unsupervised learning and reinforcement learning."}],"quiz":[{"q":"How does a computer create rules in machine learning?","options":["A developer writes them by hand","The computer works them out by itself using data and correct answers","Users input them manually","They are hardcoded into the hardware"],"answer":1,"why":"The notes state that the developer provides data and correct answers, and the computer works out the rules by itself."},{"q":"Where are the rules learned in machine learning stored?","options":["In a database","In a model","In a programmer script","In the source code"],"answer":1,"why":"The notes state that the rules it learns are stored in a model."},{"q":"What are the three main types of machine learning listed in the notes?","options":["Supervised, unsupervised, and reinforcement learning","Deep, shallow, and neural learning","Narrow, general, and super learning","Linear, logistic, and cluster learning"],"answer":0,"why":"The notes state there are three main types: supervised learning, unsupervised learning and reinforcement learning."},{"q":"What is provided by the developer in machine learning?","options":["Only the rules","Data and the correct answers","Only the source code","Pre-written models"],"answer":1,"why":"The notes state that the developer provides data and the correct answers."}],"background":"","source":{"pages":[],"excerpt":"Machine learning (ML) is a branch of AI in which a computer learns from examples instead of following rules written by a programmer.","verified":true},"id":"t2"},{"title":"Supervised Learning Fundamentals","coverage":"full","gaps":[],"explanation":"In supervised learning, training data is labelled with correct answers. The model maps features (inputs) to labels (outputs) to make predictions on unseen data. It is divided into classification, which predicts categories like spam filters, and regression, which predicts numbers like house prices. Data is typically split into training and test sets, often using an 80/20 ratio.","example":"a spam filter learns from emails labelled \"spam\" or \"not spam\" and then classifies new emails","mnemonic":"","glossary":[{"term":"Supervised Learning","meaning":"Learning from labelled training data to predict the output for new data."},{"term":"Classification","meaning":"A type of supervised learning problem that predicts a category."},{"term":"Regression","meaning":"A type of supervised learning problem that predicts a number."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is labelled data in supervised learning?","back":"Data where each example comes with the correct answer."},{"front":"What do features represent in supervised learning?","back":"The inputs to the model."},{"front":"What is the difference between classification and regression?","back":"Classification predicts a category, while regression predicts a number."},{"front":"What is the purpose of the test set?","back":"It is kept aside and used afterwards to check how well the model performs on data it has never seen."},{"front":"What is a common data split ratio for training and testing?","back":"80 percent for training and 20 percent for testing."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does a classification problem predict?","options":["A number","A category","An input","A feature"],"answer":1,"why":"Classification predicts a category, whereas regression predicts a number."},{"q":"What is the typical train/test split mentioned in the notes?","options":["50/50","70/30","80/20","90/10"],"answer":2,"why":"A common split is 80 percent for training and 20 percent for testing."},{"q":"What is regression used to predict?","options":["A category","A label","A number","A cluster"],"answer":2,"why":"Regression predicts a number, such as predicting the price of a house."},{"q":"What are the inputs called in supervised learning?","options":["Labels","Features","Clusters","Classes"],"answer":1,"why":"The inputs are called features, and the outputs are called labels."}],"background":"","source":{"pages":[],"excerpt":"In supervised learning, the training data is labelled, meaning each example comes with the correct answer. The model learns the relationship between the inputs, called features, and the output, called the label. After training, it can predict the label for new data it has not seen before.","verified":true},"id":"t3"},{"title":"Unsupervised Learning and Clustering","coverage":"partial","gaps":[],"explanation":"In unsupervised learning, data has no labels, and the model must look for structure on its own. The most common task is clustering, which groups similar items together based on patterns in the data without prior instructions on what the groups should be.","example":"a shop can cluster customers by their buying habits to find groups with similar interests, without being told in advance what the groups should be","mnemonic":"","glossary":[{"term":"Unsupervised Learning","meaning":"Learning from data that has no labels, where the model looks for structure on its own."},{"term":"Clustering","meaning":"A task that groups similar items together."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is unsupervised learning?","back":"Learning where the data has no labels and the model looks for structure on its own."},{"front":"What is the most common task in unsupervised learning?","back":"Clustering."},{"front":"What does clustering do?","back":"Groups similar items together."},{"front":"Are groups defined in advance in unsupervised clustering?","back":"No, the model finds them without being told in advance what the groups should be."}],"quiz":[{"q":"How does unsupervised learning differ from supervised learning regarding data?","options":["It uses more features","The data has no labels","It only uses regression","It requires an 80/20 split"],"answer":1,"why":"In unsupervised learning, the data has no labels and the model looks for structure on its own."},{"q":"What is the most common task in unsupervised learning?","options":["Classification","Regression","Clustering","Supervision"],"answer":2,"why":"The most common task is clustering, which groups similar items together."}],"background":"","source":{"pages":[],"excerpt":"In unsupervised learning, the data has no labels. The model looks for structure in the data on its own. The most common task is clustering, which groups similar items together.","verified":true},"id":"t4"},{"title":"Reinforcement Learning Overview","coverage":"thin","gaps":[],"explanation":"Reinforcement learning is a third type of machine learning, used in areas such as game playing and robotics.","example":"","mnemonic":"","glossary":[{"term":"Reinforcement learning","meaning":"A third type of machine learning used in areas such as game playing and robotics."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is reinforcement learning?","back":"A third type of machine learning used in areas such as game playing and robotics."},{"front":"Where is reinforcement learning used?","back":"In areas such as game playing and robotics."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What type of machine learning is reinforcement learning described as?","options":["A first type","A second type","A third type","A fourth type"],"answer":2,"why":"The notes state that reinforcement learning is a third type of machine learning."},{"q":"Which application areas are mentioned for reinforcement learning?","options":["Game playing and robotics","Image generation and translation","Database management","Web browsing and email"],"answer":0,"why":"The notes state it is used in areas such as game playing and robotics."}],"background":"","source":{"pages":[],"excerpt":"Reinforcement learning is a third type of machine learning. It is used in areas such as game playing and robotics.","verified":true},"id":"t5"},{"title":"Overfitting and Underfitting in Models","coverage":"full","gaps":[],"explanation":"A good model must work well on new data, not just training data. Overfitting occurs when a model learns training data too closely, including noise and accidental details, leading to poor performance on new data. Underfitting occurs when a model is too simple to capture the underlying pattern. Overfitting can be reduced by using more training data, simplifying the model, and stopping training early.","example":"A student who memorises past exam answers without understanding the subject is a useful comparison.","mnemonic":"","glossary":[{"term":"Overfitting","meaning":"When a model learns the training data too closely, including its noise and accidental details."},{"term":"Underfitting","meaning":"When a model is too simple to capture the pattern in the data."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is overfitting?","back":"When a model learns the training data too closely, including its noise and accidental details."},{"front":"What is underfitting?","back":"When a model is too simple to capture the pattern in the data."},{"front":"What are ways to reduce overfitting?","back":"Using more training data, simplifying the model, and stopping training early."},{"front":"What is a warning sign of overfitting?","back":"A large gap between training performance and test performance."},{"front":"What comparison is used for overfitting?","back":"A student who memorises past exam answers without understanding the subject."},{"front":"How do you check whether overfitting has occurred?","back":"By checking performance on the test set."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What causes overfitting?","options":["Learning the training data too closely including noise and accidental details","Being too simple to capture the pattern","Using too much test data","Stopping training too early"],"answer":0,"why":"Overfitting happens when a model learns the training data too closely, including its noise and accidental details."},{"q":"What happens during underfitting?","options":["The model performs well on new data","The model is too simple to capture the pattern in the data","The model learns noise and accidental details","There is a large gap between training and test performance"],"answer":1,"why":"Underfitting happens when a model is too simple to capture the pattern in the data."},{"q":"Which of the following is a way to reduce overfitting?","options":["Making the model more complex","Removing training data","Simplifying the model","Ignoring the test set"],"answer":2,"why":"Ways to reduce overfitting include using more training data, simplifying the model and stopping training early."},{"q":"What indicates that overfitting may have occurred?","options":["Equal performance on training and test sets","A large gap between training performance and test performance","Poor performance on the training set","A model that is too simple"],"answer":1,"why":"A large gap between training performance and test performance is a warning sign of overfitting."}],"background":"","source":{"pages":[],"excerpt":"Overfitting happens when a model learns the training data too closely, including its noise and accidental details, so it performs well in training but poorly on new data.","verified":true},"id":"t6"},{"title":"Evaluating a Classification Model","coverage":"partial","gaps":[],"explanation":"Accuracy is the simplest measure of a classification model, defined as the percentage of predictions that are correct. However, accuracy can be misleading when one class is much more common than the other, which is why other measures like precision and recall are used.","example":"a model that classifies 90 out of 100 emails correctly has an accuracy of 90 percent","mnemonic":"","glossary":[{"term":"Accuracy","meaning":"The percentage of predictions that are correct in a classification model."},{"term":"Precision","meaning":"Another measure used in cases where accuracy can be misleading, such as when one class is much more common than the other."},{"term":"Recall","meaning":"Another measure used when one class is much more common than the other."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the simplest measure of a classification model?","back":"Accuracy"},{"front":"How is accuracy defined?","back":"The percentage of predictions that are correct."},{"front":"When can accuracy be misleading?","back":"When one class is much more common than the other."},{"front":"What measures are used when accuracy is misleading due to unbalanced classes?","back":"Precision and recall"},{"front":"What accuracy does a model have if it classifies 90 out of 100 emails correctly?","back":"90 percent"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the simplest measure of a classification model?","options":["Accuracy","Precision","Recall","Weight"],"answer":0,"why":"The notes state that the simplest measure of a classification model is accuracy."},{"q":"When can accuracy be misleading?","options":["When all classes are equally common","When one class is much more common than the other","When there are no hidden layers","When the model uses deep learning"],"answer":1,"why":"The notes state that accuracy can be misleading when one class is much more common than the other."},{"q":"What measures are used when accuracy is misleading?","options":["Inputs and outputs","Weights and layers","Precision and recall","Training and testing"],"answer":2,"why":"The notes state that precision and recall are other measures that are used in such cases."},{"q":"What is the accuracy of a model that classifies 90 out of 100 emails correctly?","options":["80 percent","90 percent","95 percent","100 percent"],"answer":1,"why":"The notes give the example that a model classifying 90 out of 100 emails correctly has an accuracy of 90 percent."}],"background":"","source":{"pages":[],"excerpt":"The simplest measure of a classification model is accuracy, the percentage of predictions that are correct. For example, a model that classifies 90 out of 100 emails correctly has an accuracy of 90 percent. Accuracy can be misleading when one class is much more common than the other.","verified":true},"id":"t7"},{"title":"Neural Networks and Deep Learning","coverage":"full","gaps":[],"explanation":"A neural network is a model loosely inspired by the brain, made of layers of connected units called neurons with weighted connections. It consists of an input layer, one or more hidden layers, and an output layer. Deep learning uses neural networks with many hidden layers, achieving strong results in image recognition, speech recognition, and language translation, though it is expensive to train due to large data and computing needs.","example":"","mnemonic":"","glossary":[{"term":"Neural Network","meaning":"A model loosely inspired by the brain, made of layers of connected units called neurons."},{"term":"Neuron","meaning":"Connected units in the layers of a neural network."},{"term":"Weight","meaning":"A number on each connection that controls how strongly one neuron influences the next."},{"term":"Deep Learning","meaning":"Uses neural networks with many hidden layers."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a neural network loosely inspired by?","back":"The brain"},{"front":"What are the connected units in a neural network called?","back":"Neurons"},{"front":"What does a weight control in a neural network?","back":"How strongly one neuron influences the next"},{"front":"What are the three types of layers mentioned in a neural network?","back":"Input layer, hidden layers, and output layer"},{"front":"What is deep learning?","back":"Neural networks with many hidden layers"},{"front":"What areas has deep learning achieved strong results in?","back":"Image recognition, speech recognition and language translation"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What controls how strongly one neuron influences the next in a neural network?","options":["A bias","A weight","An activation function","An output"],"answer":1,"why":"The notes state that each connection has a weight, which is a number that controls how strongly one neuron influences the next."},{"q":"Which layer receives the data in a neural network?","options":["Output layer","Hidden layer","Input layer","Training layer"],"answer":2,"why":"The notes state that the network has an input layer that receives the data."},{"q":"What defines deep learning according to the notes?","options":["Neural networks with many hidden layers","Models that use high accuracy","Models that use precision and recall","Networks with no weights"],"answer":0,"why":"The notes state that deep learning uses neural networks with many hidden layers."},{"q":"Why is deep learning expensive to train?","options":["It requires human supervision at all times","It usually needs large amounts of data and a lot of computing power","It only works on email classification","It lacks an input layer"],"answer":1,"why":"The notes state that deep learning usually needs large amounts of data and a lot of computing power, which makes it expensive to train."}],"background":"","source":{"pages":[],"excerpt":"A neural network is a model loosely inspired by the brain. It is made of layers of connected units called neurons. Each connection has a weight, which is a number that controls how strongly one neuron influences the next. The network has an input layer that receives the data, one or more hidden layers that process it, and an output layer that gives the result.","verified":true},"id":"t8"},{"title":"Preparing Data for Machine Learning","coverage":"partial","gaps":[],"explanation":"Data preparation is a crucial step because model quality depends directly on data quality. Real-world data frequently includes missing values, mistakes, and duplicates, requiring thorough cleaning prior to training. Additionally, features often need to be scaled to prevent large numbers from dominating smaller ones. In practice, this preparation phase frequently consumes more time than actually building the model.","example":"","mnemonic":"","glossary":[{"term":"Data cleaning","meaning":"The process of addressing missing values, mistakes, and duplicates in real data before training a model."},{"term":"Feature scaling","meaning":"Adjusting features so that large numbers do not dominate small ones."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why must real data be cleaned before training?","back":"Because real data often contains missing values, mistakes, and duplicates."},{"front":"Why do features need to be scaled?","back":"So that large numbers do not dominate small ones."},{"front":"What part of the machine learning process often takes more time than building the model?","back":"Data preparation."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What can happen if features are not scaled?","options":["Large numbers may dominate small ones","The model will have no missing values","Data preparation will take less time","Duplicates will be automatically removed"],"answer":0,"why":"The notes state that features may need to be scaled so that large numbers do not dominate small ones."},{"q":"What issues are commonly found in real data?","options":["Missing values, mistakes, and duplicates","Only formatting errors","Perfect accuracy and complete records","Pre-scaled features"],"answer":0,"why":"The notes state that real data often contains missing values, mistakes and duplicates."}],"background":"","source":{"pages":[],"excerpt":"Real data often contains missing values, mistakes and duplicates, so it must be cleaned before training.","verified":true},"id":"t9"},{"title":"Ethics in Artificial Intelligence","coverage":"partial","gaps":[],"explanation":"AI systems can cause harm if built carelessly. Key ethical considerations include bias, where training data that fails to fairly represent all groups leads unfair treatment; privacy, as systems trained on personal data must protect it; and explainability, which is the ability to understand why a model made a decision.","example":"a hiring tool trained mostly on past applications from one group may favour that group.","mnemonic":"","glossary":[{"term":"Bias","meaning":"A risk where a model treats some groups unfairly because the training data does not fairly represent all groups of people."},{"term":"Privacy","meaning":"A concern regarding the protection of personal data used to train AI systems."},{"term":"Explainability","meaning":"The ability to understand why a model made a decision."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What causes bias in an AI model according to the notes?","back":"When training data does not fairly represent all groups of people."},{"front":"What is explainability in AI?","back":"The ability to understand why a model made a decision."},{"front":"Why is privacy a concern in AI systems?","back":"Because many AI systems are trained on personal data and must protect it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What ethical concern involves understanding why a model made a decision?","options":["Explainability","Bias","Privacy","Data scaling"],"answer":0,"why":"Explainability is defined in the notes as the ability to understand why a model made a decision."},{"q":"According to the notes, what can cause a model to treat some groups unfairly?","options":["Training data that does not fairly represent all groups of people","Scaling features incorrectly","Spending too much time building the model","Removing duplicates from the dataset"],"answer":0,"why":"The notes state that bias is a risk if the training data does not fairly represent all groups of people."}],"background":"","source":{"pages":[],"excerpt":"Bias is one risk: if the training data does not fairly represent all groups of people, the model can treat some groups unfairly.","verified":true},"id":"t10"}]}